  "query_preview": "..\n\nSurface cold front finishes crossing lower MI this evening with only \na low chance for an isolated brief shower as primary moisture axis \nhas shifted over the eastern Great Lakes. Post-frontal cold \nadvection in place through the night into Monday as upper troughing \nsettles over the eastern half of the Great Lakes. Core of the \ncoolest air arrives Monday keeping tonight's lows in the upper 30-\nlower 40 range precluding a risk of frost. Local 850mb temps fall \ntowards 0C by midday Monday capping high temps around 60 despite \nbuilding high pressure promoting mostly sunny skies. Said high \ndrifts directly overhead by Monday night leading to calm or near \ncalm winds under clear skies allowing temperatures outside of the \nurban centers to fall into the 30s. Coldest temps expected across \nthe Saginaw Valley and Thumb where mid 30s are likely offering \npotential for areas of frost to develop by Tuesday morning. \n\nHigh pressure departs for the mid-Atlantic daytime Tuesday as broad \nlow pressure dives southeast across northern Ontario setting up \nsouth-southwest return flow across southern lower MI. Low pressure \nlooks to track far enough north that SE MI stays dry save for \nperhaps a stray shower towards the tip of the Thumb Tuesday night. \nWith the area currently favored to reside on the southernmost fringe \nof the James Bay upper low, temperatures throughout the midweek \nperiod would hold around the seasonal average, in the mid-upper 60s, \nwith Wednesday making a run towards 70.",
